@meta
id: 14-19
title: Number the steps
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-expression
par: 40
minutes: 6
vscode: retype seven bullets as numbers, then renumber after inserting one
open: RUNBOOK.md

@brief
The rollback runbook's steps are bullets, and the on-call engineer
skipped one last week because bullets do not say "in this order".
Turn the seven bullets into a numbered list, `1.` through `7.`, and
leave the prose around them alone.

An expression replacement can see more than the match. `line('.')`
is the number of the line the match is on, and the bullets start on
line 6. A little subtraction gives you the step number for free, and
the result stays correct however many steps there are.

@keys
| `\=line('.')` | the current line number, as the replacement |
| `\=line('.')-5 . '. '` | that number minus five, then a dot and a space — `.` joins strings |
| `^- ` | a bullet at the start of a line — only the bullets match |

@hints
The pattern is the bullet marker anchored to the start of the line.
The replacement is an expression built from the line number.
--
The first bullet is on line 6 and should become `1. `, so the number
you want is the line number minus five. Join it to the text `'. '`
with the `.` operator.
--
`:%s/^- /\=line('.')-5 . '. '/` and Enter. The range can be `%`
because no other line starts with a bullet.

@solution
    :%s/^- /\=line('.')-5 . '. '/<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-four keystrokes for seven numbers you did not count.

`line('.')` inside a `\=` replacement is evaluated separately for each
match, with the cursor on that match's line, so it counts up as the
substitute walks down the file. Subtracting the offset of the block is
the usual trick. Chapter 25 will give you a way to avoid the
arithmetic, and chapter 21's macros can renumber too, but this is the
version that fits in one command and needs nothing you do not already
have.

The `.` between the number and the string is vim's concatenation
operator, and the spaces around it matter: `5.'. '` can confuse the
parser into looking for a decimal. Newer vim also accepts `..`, which
is unambiguous and worth adopting. Either way, the expression is
ordinary vimscript, and everything you learn about it in chapter 24
will work inside a substitute.

@start RUNBOOK.md
# Rolling back a bad deploy

Run these in order. Do not skip the queue check; a rollback with
messages in flight loses them.

- Announce the rollback in #payments-oncall
- Pause the refund consumers: `./stop/refund-worker`
- Wait for the queue to drain: `./scripts/queue-depth payments.refunds`
- Roll the image back: `./deploy/rollback order-service`
- Restart the consumers: `./start/refund-worker`
- Confirm the version: `curl -s gateway.internal/health`
- Close the incident and note the version you rolled back to

The steps are not optional and the order is not negotiable.

@expected RUNBOOK.md
# Rolling back a bad deploy

Run these in order. Do not skip the queue check; a rollback with
messages in flight loses them.

1. Announce the rollback in #payments-oncall
2. Pause the refund consumers: `./stop/refund-worker`
3. Wait for the queue to drain: `./scripts/queue-depth payments.refunds`
4. Roll the image back: `./deploy/rollback order-service`
5. Restart the consumers: `./start/refund-worker`
6. Confirm the version: `curl -s gateway.internal/health`
7. Close the incident and note the version you rolled back to

The steps are not optional and the order is not negotiable.
